      "content": "I apologize for the excessive goblin metaphors earlier; I understand you just need clear, practical tax advice without the extra noise.\n\nRegarding your ergonomic chair: Since it is a significant purchase, you generally cannot deduct the full cost as a regular business expense in the year you buy it. Instead, you must capitalize it and depreciate it over time.\n\nFor tax purposes, office furniture like a chair typically falls under the **5-year property class** (for tax years after 2017). This means you can deduct a portion of the cost each year using the **Straight-Line depreciation method**.\n\nHere is the breakdown:\n*   **Year 1:** You can deduct approximately **20%** of the chair's cost as a depreciation expense.\n*   **Years 2–5:** You will deduct the remaining **80%** in equal installments over the next four years.\n\n**Important Note:** If you choose to use the **Bonus Depreciation** election (which is currently available for many assets), you might be able to deduct **100%** of the chair's cost in the year you purchase it. However, this election is subject to change based on current tax laws, so you should",
